@flexiarg interface/write-for-the-read-aloud-loop
@title Write for the Read-Aloud Loop
@status accepted 2026-11-11: proposed by 象 for 2 turns (Joe 2026-11-11: recurrence suffices); attested once used in a mission or a War Machine cascade
@provenance 象 proposals, ~/.emacs-graph/session-turn-analysis/<record>[.json].candidates.json; records: turn-ElMAv4 turn-yXlhrr
@parent writing-coherence/meet-the-reader-where-they-are
@context Joe interacts with agents through a dictation/TTS loop where text is read aloud to him.

! conclusion: State the interface constraint explicitly as a procedural note and name the formatting rule the agent must follow (keep a paragraph in one block) so the output survives the read-aloud path.

  + IF:
    A rendering step silently drops or mangles some formatting (here: anything after a line break is not read out).

  + HOWEVER:
    The constraint lives in the operator's tooling, not in anything the agent can discover from the text itself; undiscovered, output keeps being half-heard.

  + THEN:
    State the interface constraint explicitly as a procedural note and name the formatting rule the agent must follow (keep a paragraph in one block) so the output survives the read-aloud path.

  + BECAUSE:
    Meet-the-reader generalises to the literal reader: the 'reader' is a speech synthesizer with a hard formatting constraint, and meeting it where it is means shaping paragraphs to survive it.
